    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters May 10th with a clear hierarchy in offensive production. Our highest-rated play is **Trevor Story** (70.40), who draws a favorable matchup against N. Martinez (xERA 4.56); his ability to leverage a +7% park factor and a +13% SP skill advantage makes him our primary anchor. Not far behind is **Corbin Carroll** (70.14). Despite facing an elite hold pitcher in the opposing dugout, **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start due to his massive speed upside, which remains the cornerstone of our team strategy. **Josh Naylor** (67.77) and **James Wood** (67.08) also provide high-floor stability, with **James Wood** capitalizing on a prime lead-off spot (+10% order bonus) and a favorable xWOBA of .377 against S. Alcantara.\n\nThe decision to keep **Travis Bazzana** (44.91) on the bench was the most difficult calculation of the morning, as his strong .382 xWOBA nearly pushed him into the starting rotation. However, **Travis Bazzana** fell short due to the cumulative weight of an unfavorable SP matchup and a bottom-third batting order penalty, which squeezed his efficiency score below that of our starters. We are also monitoring risks with **Seiya Suzuki** (43.27), whose score is significantly suppressed by the daunting presence of deGrom on the mound; his -19% SP skill penalty makes him a marginal play today. Furthermore, lineup uncertainty persists for **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Alex Freeland**, **Austin Riley**, **Corbin Carroll**, and **Zach McKinstry**. We are tentatively starting this group based on historical batting order data, but any unexpected shifts in the official lineups could necessitate a recalibration to protect our daily game caps. **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** round out our top-tier options, with **Tyler Soderstrom** specifically benefiting from a massive +16% park factor that boosts his overall contribution despite his platoon disadvantage.",
